A||||6||Wrench||Front Shock Removal||||1||
Z||000000||Wrench||11-25-2001||09:02 AM||||Any tips to removing the front shocks on an 86 GT. The lower shock nut comes off fine but the bolt is stuck in the steel bushing sleeve. Can't get a punch even close to knock the bolt out because the coil spring is in the way. Is a torch the only way out?<BR>Any help appreciated...... Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||11-25-2001||09:46 AM||||Using a tourch on the shock is not recommended, but you do have the right idea, you need to cut the bottem "eye lit" and spread it to expose the inner sleve.  <P>I would soak it with penatrating oil and keep on pounding a bit more.  likely U have been doing that.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||11-25-2001||12:54 PM||||Bet you can get a large center punch in there.  I just used a lot of penetrating oil and kept unscrewing it with my socket.  It worked its way out eventually.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-25-2001||01:53 PM||||if the sleeve rotates, spin it around until you find the seam in it. Use a big wrench to tear it loose if needed.<P>Then whack that seam with a center punch or punch right next to it. (Dpends just how the seam is formed which one works better.)Do that to both ends of the sleeve. that will spread the sleeve and let oil get in better and eaze pressure on the accumulated rust.<P>before you put the new shocks on grease the inside of the sleeve and the bolt shanks to prevent them seizing again. put anti sieze on the top bolts.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wrench||11-25-2001||06:45 PM||||Thanks for all the info. I'll give it a few more "wacks" this week.<P>     Thanks again folks!<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000005||Eriamjh||11-26-2001||08:12 AM||||If worse come to worse, grind off the bolt head and drive the stud through the other end with an air hammer.<P>And then grease the sh*t out of everything before you put it back together.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fee-air-oh||11-26-2001||09:44 AM||||Just did that 2 days ago.I used a big steel clamp ,put the nonmovable side of clamp on the nut side with the nut put back on for more surface area,I also put an oversize socket on the otherside (toward rear of car)so when I turned the clamp the bolt would slide into the socket.Basically pressing the bolt out of the rusted sleeve.<BR>    This worked right away for me, good luck.||||reg||1
